
    tclpdf - PDF generation for Tcl


    This directory contains a freely distributable pure tcl extension called tclpdf.


SYNOPSIS

    Create PDF documents from Tcl: pages, graphics, embedded fonts, images,
    tables and electronic invoices.

    Beyond the usual page, text and graphics work it does two things a PDF
    writer often leaves out: general file attachments, and form XObjects that
    store a drawing once and place it as often as wanted.

        Document and graphics
            Pages and the five page boxes, paths, clipping to a rectangle or
            an arbitrary path, transparency and blend modes, form XObjects for
            reusable content, tiling patterns, axial and radial shadings,
            RGB / CMYK / grey / separation colours and 148 colour names
            without Tk.

        Text
            Single lines and paragraphs with justification, indents, a height
            limit that hands back what did not fit, text flowing around
            shapes, soft hyphens, leader rows, text along a path and page
            numbers. Right-to-left lines are set as well: Hebrew by order
            alone, Arabic with its contextual forms from the face's own GSUB
            tables, with the numbers inside such a line keeping their own
            order and paired brackets mirrored. What cannot be set correctly
            is refused rather than drawn wrong.

        Fonts
            The fourteen standard faces, and three formats to embed: TrueType
            as Type0/CIDFontType2 with Identity-H and a ToUnicode CMap, subset
            down to the glyphs actually used, a variable face at any point on
            its axes; OpenType with CFF outlines and Type 1 whole, since
            subsetting either would need outlines this package does not read.
            Pair kerning and the standard ligatures are applied from the
            font's own tables, both on by default. A missing character raises
            an error instead of silently disappearing.

        Images
            JPEG and PNG. Both are passed through unchanged wherever possible:
            a JPEG stream goes in as /DCTDecode, a PNG without alpha as
            /FlateDecode with /Predictor 15 - neither is ever decoded. PNG
            alpha becomes a soft mask.

        Tables
            Column widths, cell wrapping, page breaks with repeated header and
            footer rows, a table too wide for the page dealt over further
            pages, rowSpan/colSpan, decimal alignment and per-cell hooks.

        SVG
            Shapes, paths, transforms, groups, reuse, viewBox and gradients,
            drawn as PDF vectors rather than as a picture. Text in a drawing
            uses the same faces as the rest of the document, embedded ones
            included.

        Barcodes
            Through tzint, which encodes into SVG that tclpdf then draws as
            vectors. No encoder of its own, and none needed; tzint is
            optional.

        Tagged PDF and PDF/A-3a
            A structure tree with the 49 standard types, nesting checked
            against Annex L, artifacts kept out of it and named by kind, and
            abbreviations carrying their expansion.

        Accessibility
            On top of the structure tree, PDF/UA-1 and PDF/UA-2 with the
            Well-Tagged PDF declaration. The claim is refused when the
            document does not keep it: a title, a language, every font
            embedded including the standard fourteen, headings without a gap,
            tables with the same number of cells in every row, a description
            on every link, every picture either described or declared
            decoration. All of them are reported at once when the file is
            written, each naming the call to change.

        Navigation and presentation
            Links to a URL, a page or a structure element, bookmarks, page
            labels, viewer preferences, and file attachments with their own
            relationship, description and date.

        Electronic invoices
            ZUGFeRD / Factur-X as PDF/A-3B: ICC profile, OutputIntent, XMP with
            the Factur-X extension schema, embedded XML, file specification,
            /AF entry and name tree. The conformance level is derived from
            BT-24 of the invoice XML rather than taken on trust.

    Everything listed above is built and tested; nothing here is planned or
    partial. Measured against the page count of its chapters, that covers
    roughly a third of ISO 32000-1 - and the missing two thirds are almost
    entirely what a READER of foreign PDFs needs rather than a writer, plus
    encryption and form fields. See the manual for the complete list of
    commands.


DEPENDENCIES

    Tcl >= 8.6.11 (runs under Tcl 9 as well)

    The core needs nothing beyond Tcl itself: zlib is a built-in command,
    not a package.

        tdom     - required for the XMP metadata packet, so every document
                   that declares PDF/A, PDF/UA or ZUGFeRD needs it. It is
                   also what reads SVG when present: it parses 27 to 48 times
                   faster than the parser tclpdf brings along and refuses an
                   entity expansion bomb. Without it SVG still works, through
                   the built-in element tree parser, and a document with no
                   metadata claim runs without tdom.

        tzint    - optional, only for barcodes. It produces SVG, which
                   "svg -data" draws; no code in this package knows about
                   barcodes.

    That is the whole list. Encryption is planned work and will name its
    package here once it exists.

    Tk is not required and must not be pulled in.


GETTING THE CODE

    The development repository is hosted at https://fossil.sowaswie.de/tclpdf
    You are encouraged to use trunk.


INSTALLING

    Simply extract the download tclpdf-<version>.zip into your Tcl library path.

    Since tclpdf is TEA-compatible you should be able to do

        ./configure
        make test
        make install

    Build IN the source directory. A separate build directory configures
    cleanly but cannot run the tests: the generated pkgIndex.tcl locates the
    modules through $dir, which is then the build directory, while the 67
    .tcl files stay where the sources are. Nothing is lost by building
    in-tree - make install puts index and modules together again either way.

    By default this installs into the lib directory of the Tcl
    installation found by configure (use --with-tcl to point it to the
    tclConfig.sh of another Tcl), so that this tclsh finds the package
    on its auto_path. To install into a different location use

        ./configure --prefix /path/to/tree

    which installs the package into /path/to/tree/lib/tclpdf<version>.


LICENCE, AND WHY NO FONT IS PART OF IT

    tclpdf itself is MIT - see the file license.terms. That covers everything
    the package installs: the Tcl modules, pkgIndex.tcl and the sRGB ICC
    profile in icc/, which is a runtime part because its bytes end up inside
    every PDF/A document.

    ONE OTHER PIECE OF THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL travels in the source archive:
    tools/agl/glyphlist.txt, the Adobe Glyph List, verbatim and under Adobe's
    own BSD licence, whose text is the header of the file. It is BUILD-TIME
    data - tools/mkafm.tcl turns glyph names into the widths in afmData.tcl
    with it - and it is not installed. Before 2026-08-13 the same table was
    read out of a pdf4tcl installation instead, which made afmData.tcl
    unreproducible without another project on the disk.

    NO FONT IS PART OF THE PACKAGE. The faces under examples/assets/fonts are
    there so that the tests and the examples have something to embed, and they
    travel in the source archive alone:

        make install                    installs no font
        tclpdf<version>.zip, .tar.gz    contain no font
        tclpdf<version>-src.tar.gz      carries them, with their licences

    They are third-party work under their own terms: DejaVu under the
    Bitstream Vera licence, and Roboto, Bitcount Prop Single and Niconne under
    the SIL Open Font License 1.1. The full texts sit beside them in
    examples/assets/fonts/licenses/, each named after the family it belongs
    to, and they have to travel with any copy of those files. The inventory in
    examples/assets/README.md records for every face where it came from, under
    what terms, and what its embedding flag says.

    Whether you may embed a font into a PDF that tclpdf writes is a question
    for THAT font's licence, not for this one. All four here allow it without
    restriction; a face you add yourself may not, and tclpdf will not stop you
    - it reads the fsType flag and reports it, but does not refuse on it,
    because no validator and no reader enforces it either and a package that
    silently declined a licensed face would be the more damaging mistake.


REPORTING BUGS

    Please head to https://fossil.sowaswie.de/tclpdf/ticket and
    click on "New Ticket". Log in as anonymous and report your findings.
    If you prefer to have an individual login write Alex a mail.


